# PHASE 7 — ANIMATION ENGINEERING AND ACCESSIBILITY

### Technical requirements

- Reuse the existing ScrollReveal component if it can support named animation variants.
- Refactor it if necessary so it does not force one generic animation.
- Use the existing animation libraries and utilities.
- Do not install another animation library without demonstrating why it is required.
- Use IntersectionObserver only in the web-specific implementation where appropriate.
- Use existing native-compatible animation capabilities for native environments.
- Clean up observers, event listeners and animation subscriptions.
- Avoid duplicate triggers.
- Prevent layout shifts.
- Prefer opacity and transform animations.
- Avoid continuous expensive layout calculations.
- Avoid excessive blur and large-area filters.
- Avoid unnecessary pinned sections.
- Avoid aggressive parallax.
- Avoid animation that blocks clicks, scrolling or keyboard interaction.

### Motion accessibility

Support:

- `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Native reduced-motion preferences where supported.
- Static or subtle animation fallbacks.
- Keyboard navigation.
- Screen-reader accessibility.
- Visible content even when animations fail or are disabled.

For reduced-motion users:

- Disable large parallax effects.
- Disable elaborate clip-path sequences where necessary.
- Remove large scale transitions.
- Replace complex motion with subtle opacity or immediate visibility.
- Never hide important content until an animation finishes.

### Performance profiles

Desktop:
- Full animation experience where performance allows.

Tablet:
- Moderate parallax and simplified decorative motion.

Mobile:
- Fewer floating objects.
- Simplified reveals.
- No pointer-based interactions.
- Minimal continuous animations.

